<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="The story of Stella Veneta, the Italian Michelin family and the name that later became MICHE."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/stories/michelin-navnet/michelin-aftalen.webp"/><title>When the Michelin name nearly became a problem · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head>
<body><EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath="/historier/da-michelin-navnet-blev-et-problem/"/><main><article class="anecdote-page">
<header class="anecdote-hero"><div class="bike-kicker"><a href={`${base}/en/stories/`}>← Stories and anecdotes</a><span>Veneto · Italy</span></div><p class="section-label">One name, two families</p><h1>When the Michelin name<br/>nearly became <em>a problem</em></h1><p class="anecdote-deck">My Stella Veneta carries a name with a rather larger history than one might expect: Michelin.</p></header>
<figure class="anecdote-image"><img src={image} alt="Illustration of the agreement between the Italian and French Michelin families concerning the name MICHE"/><figcaption>Illustration of the story handed down about the naming agreement. The image is not a historical document.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="anecdote-layout"><aside class="anecdote-timeline" aria-label="Timeline"><div><strong>1919</strong><span>Ciclo Piave founded</span></div><div><strong>1936</strong><span>Stella Veneta established</span></div><div><strong>1948</strong><span>F.M.C. created</span></div><div><strong>1970</strong><span>Name changed to F.A.C. di Michelin</span></div><div><strong>1986</strong><span>MICHE brand introduced</span></div></aside>
<div class="anecdote-copy"><p class="dropcap">But this is not France and Bibendum’s tyre company. This is Veneto in northern Italy.</p>
<h2>From Ciclo Piave to Stella Veneta</h2><p>In 1919, brothers Augusto and Ferdinando Michelin founded the bicycle factory Ciclo Piave in Santa Lucia di Piave. In 1936 they went their separate ways, and Ferdinando established a new factory in Conegliano. It was named Stella Veneta — roughly “the star of Veneto”. The factory built both bicycles and small motorcycles.</p>
<div class="anecdote-pullquote">A surname that could open doors — and cause trouble.</div>
<h2>Changing gear with both hands</h2><p>My Stella Veneta is fitted with one of the period’s most fascinating gear systems: the Campagnolo Cambio Corsa. A gear change did not involve a small lever. The rider operated two long rods beside the rear wheel, released the hub, moved the chain and at the same time guided the wheel into the correct position.</p><p>The Cambio Corsa belongs to the brief transition before the modern rear derailleur took over. It is a system that makes the gear change visible: mechanics, balance and timing combined in one manoeuvre.</p>
<h2>A new company and an old name</h2><p>In 1948, Ferdinando Michelin also established the component company F.M.C. — Ferdinando Michelin Conegliano. It made locks, luggage racks and wheel quick-releases, among other items. The name changed to F.A.C. di Michelin in 1970, and in 1986 the company introduced the MICHE brand.</p>
<h2>The name change is written on the pedal</h2><p>The collection includes an Italian pedal from before MICHE became the new brand. The pedal body bears the name <strong>MICHELIN</strong>. It appears again on the pedal plate, just above the words <em>Made in Italy</em>.</p><p>It is a small but tangible detail. The change did not take place only on letterheads and company registers. Older components left the factory with the family’s full surname cast and stamped directly into the metal. Later, they said MICHE.</p>
<figure class="collection-detail"><img src={pedalImage} alt="Italian racing bicycle pedal from the collection with MICHELIN stamped on its body and plate"/><figcaption>Pedal from the collection bearing the MICHELIN name, from before the brand was shortened to MICHE. Photograph: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>The agreement that did not go to court</h2><p>Here, the documented company history gives way to family anecdote. According to the Michelin family’s own account, the French tyre company reacted when the Italians began using the name internationally.</p><p>The matter is said to have ended not in court but with an almost old-fashioned <em>gentlemen’s agreement</em>: the Italians could use the name MICHE if they promised never to make tyres. A letter from the French Michelin company is reportedly still held in the family archive.</p>
<div class="source-note"><strong>What do we know for certain?</strong><p>MICHE’s official history documents Ciclo Piave in 1919, Stella Veneta in 1936, F.M.C. in 1948, F.A.C. di Michelin in 1970 and the introduction of the MICHE name in 1986. The gentlemen’s agreement itself, and the promise never to make tyres, are a family account not documented in publicly available primary sources.</p></div>
<h2>The name lives on</h2><p>MICHE still exists as an Italian bicycle-component manufacturer. In 2022, F.A.C. Michelin became part of the group surrounding Wilier Triestina.</p><p>There are also stories that the Italian Michelin family and the French tyre family were distantly related. I have not been able to document that with confidence. What is documented is nearly as good: the two Michelin names came close enough that their identities had to be negotiated.</p>
